#!/usr/bin/env python3
"""dev - the single developer entry point for the Cheat Downloader pak.

Everything a developer (or CI) needs lives here: fetching prerequisites,
building for the host and for devices, running the test suites, packaging
releases, and editing the version/changelog in pak.json.

    ./dev setup            # download compilers, sources, and helper binaries
    ./dev build            # host build (fast, for local testing)
    ./dev build all        # cross-build every device platform
    ./dev test             # unit tests
    ./dev test --e2e       # unit + end-to-end tests
    ./dev devices          # list connected ADB devices and their platforms
    ./dev install device   # build and install onto every connected device
    ./dev run              # build and run against a sandbox SD card
    ./dev run device       # build, install, and launch on a connected device
    ./dev dist --strict    # build and package the release artifacts
    ./dev clean            # remove generated files
    ./dev version latest   # inspect or edit pak.json versions
    ./dev docker tg5040    # shell into a cross-compilation toolchain

How the build fits together
---------------------------
The app is written in Nim and compiles down to C, so a build needs three
things: the Nim compiler, the miniz C sources that get compiled into the
binary, and (for devices) an ARM64 cross-toolchain. Only the toolchain is
"installed" - it is a container image pulled on demand. The Nim compiler and
miniz are unpacked under workspace/, and the prebuilt minui-list and
minui-presenter helper binaries land in deps/. Both directories are
gitignored and fully reproducible from `./dev setup`.

Host builds run directly on this machine and produce ./cheat_manager, which
is what the test suites exercise. Device builds run the same Nim compiler
inside the platform's toolchain container, which mounts workspace/ as its
working directory - that is why the sources are copied into workspace/ and
why every compiler path below is written relative to workspace/.

Devices are reached over ADB. `devices` reports what is connected and which
platform each one is, and any command that takes a target accepts `device`
(every connected device), a platform name, or an ADB serial.
"""

